### The key ideas at a glance

1. **A Dec-POMDP** has a hidden state, joint actions, private observations and one reward $R(s, \boldsymbol{a})$ that every agent maximizes. It is a POMG whose agents all have that same reward.

2. **Cooperation doesn't remove the hard part.** Each agent acts on its own observation history, without knowing the others'. Policies are again conditional plans or controllers. Finding the optimal joint policy is NEXP-complete (D. S. Bernstein et al., 2002), harder than POMDPs.

3. **Structure helps.** Subclasses make it easier: if the joint observations reveal the state (Dec-MDP); if the state, transitions, observations and rewards factor per agent; or if observations can be shared freely (an MPOMDP, which is just a big POMDP). Sharing observations gives an **upper bound** on what the team can do.

4. **Search.** Dynamic programming with pruning (Chapter 26) is exact but can prune little when rewards are shared. **Iterated best response** (JESP) improves one agent at a time; it converges to a joint policy no single agent can improve, which may be far from the best. **Memory-bounded search** (MBDP) keeps a fixed number of plans per agent, chosen at beliefs sampled by simulation.

5. **Controllers** of fixed size handle infinite horizons. They can be optimized by nonlinear programming, which can also stop at a poor local optimum.

### Vocabulary

| Term | Plain meaning |
|:---|:---|
| Dec-POMDP | A team problem: shared reward, private observations |
| Dec-MDP | A Dec-POMDP whose agents' observations, pooled, would reveal the state |
| MMDP / MPOMDP | Multiagent MDP / POMDP: the team shares everything, so it's one big MDP / POMDP |
| Factored model | State split into one part per agent (plus the environment) |
| JESP | Joint equilibrium-based search for policies: iterated best response for teams |
| MBDP | Memory-bounded dynamic programming: keep a few plans per agent |
| Value of communication | How much more the team could earn by sharing observations |

